<link rel="stylesheet" href="css/site-layout.css?v=20261010-layout2"></head><body><?php require __DIR__ . '/includes/site-header.php'; ?><main class="shell legal-page"><section class="intro"><h1>Risk Disclosure & Disclaimer</h1><p>Important risks associated with tokenized real-world assets, blockchain transactions, data and AI-assisted analysis.</p><div class="legal-effective">Effective date: October 8, 2026</div></section>
<div class="legal-callout"><strong>Important:</strong> Tokenized real-world assets and blockchain transactions can involve substantial risk, including loss of some or all of the value committed. This page is a general risk summary, not a complete list.</div>
<section class="legal-card"><h2>1. Tokenized asset and issuer risk</h2><p>A token that references a stock, fund, commodity, receivable or other real-world asset may not provide the same legal rights, custody protections, voting rights, redemption rights, dividends, corporate-action treatment or regulatory status as direct ownership of the underlying asset. Rights depend on the token issuer/provider and its legal structure.</p></section>
<section class="legal-card"><h2>2. Price and reference-price risk</h2><p>Token prices may diverge from the referenced real-world asset because of market hours, liquidity, provider mechanics, oracle/reference data, fees, settlement delays, corporate actions, market closures or blockchain-specific trading conditions. A recently fetched API response does not guarantee that the underlying source price itself is current.</p></section>
<section class="legal-card"><h2>3. Liquidity, RFQ and execution risk</h2><p>A token can appear supported while a current executable quote is unavailable. RFQ liquidity can disappear quickly. Quotes can expire within seconds, minimum order requirements can apply, and execution may fail because of liquidity, slippage, route changes, allowance, gas or provider conditions.</p></section>
<section class="legal-card"><h2>4. Smart-contract and blockchain risk</h2><p>Smart contracts, routers, tokens and blockchain infrastructure can contain bugs, design flaws, upgrade risks or malicious behaviour. Transactions may be irreversible. Network congestion, reorganization, RPC failures or gas-price changes can delay or prevent execution.</p></section>
<section class="legal-card"><h2>5. Wallet and approval risk</h2><p>Token approvals grant a spender permission over the approved token amount. Verify the token, spender and exact amount in your wallet. A compromised device, malicious wallet extension, phishing site or mistaken signature can result in loss. BNB RWA Hub will never need your seed phrase or private key.</p></section>
<section class="legal-card"><h2>6. Stablecoin and settlement risk</h2><p>Trades may use USDT or another settlement asset. Stablecoins can lose their intended peg, face issuer, reserve, regulatory, liquidity or smart-contract risks, and should not be treated as risk-free cash.</p></section>
<section class="legal-card"><h2>7. Data, model and software risk</h2><p>Saved market data may be incomplete, stale or mapped incorrectly. Technical indicators are deterministic calculations over the stored data but do not predict future performance. Hubble is an AI system and can misunderstand requests, omit context or generate incorrect analysis. Software safeguards and simulations reduce some failure modes but cannot guarantee successful or economically favourable execution.</p></section>
<section class="legal-card"><h2>8. Provider and infrastructure risk</h2><p>Binance Web3 APIs, wallet providers, RPC endpoints, AI/model providers and token issuers are third parties. Rate limits, compliance restrictions, downtime, changed APIs or provider-specific conditions may interrupt research or trading workflows.</p></section>
<section class="legal-card"><h2>9. Regulatory and tax risk</h2><p>Tokenized assets can be regulated differently across jurisdictions and rules may change. Access, transferability, reporting, taxes and investor eligibility can depend on your location and circumstances. You are responsible for obtaining professional advice where needed.</p></section>
<section class="legal-card"><h2>10. Demo limits are not risk controls</h2><p>The site's purchase and sale limits are product demonstration constraints. They are not a statement that an amount within those limits is safe, suitable or affordable for you.</p></section>
<section class="legal-card"><h2>11. Verify before signing</h2><p>Before any wallet confirmation, verify the asset, provider, amount, minimum receive amount, spender/router, network and gas. If anything is unclear, reject the wallet request and investigate before trying again.</p></section>
